# .NET
bin/
obj/
*.user
*.suo
[Tt]est[Rr]esults/

# Godot 4
.godot/
.import/
export.cfg
# NOTE: export_presets.cfg IS tracked (the windows-client/linux-dedicated presets carry no
# secrets — no keystores/credentials; Godot keeps those in export_credentials.cfg, which stays
# ignored via .godot/).
mono_crash.*.json
# NOTE: *.cs.uid files are intentionally committed (Godot 4.4+ guidance) for
# stable script UIDs across machines/checkouts. Do NOT add *.uid here.

# IDE
.vs/
.idea/
.vscode/

# Visual-capture output (--screenshot; see docs/RUNNING.md "Visual capture"). The
# .gdignore IS tracked so the Godot editor skips this tree (no *.import sidecars
# for captured PNGs) even on a fresh checkout before anything is captured.
screenshots/*
!screenshots/.gdignore
# Stray captures dropped in the project root (prefer _scratch/ below).
_*.png

# Local scratch / throwaway dev files (test textures, comparison captures, one-off
# experiments). The folder is .gdignore'd so
# Godot never imports it (no *.import noise) and the contents are git-ignored. The
# two marker files stay tracked so a fresh checkout has the folder ready to use.
_scratch/*
!_scratch/.gdignore
!_scratch/README.md

# Export/packaging output (export presets + tools/package.sh). Test-run artifacts
# (TestResults/) are already covered by the [Tt]est[Rr]esults/ rule above.
/dist/

# Per-machine ProjectSettings overrides, e.g. `./vx build --no-render-thread`. Godot merges this over
# project.godot when it RUNS the project and ignores it when the editor exports, so it is a local fallback
# by construction — and untracked on purpose, because a machine-specific workaround riding along in someone
# else's commit is precisely what having a separate file avoids.
/override.cfg

# Repo-local Godot install. tools/lib/find-godot.sh probes this BEFORE PATH, so a clone can pin its own
# engine without touching the machine and two clones can disagree about the version; `./vx setup` will
# install here. Never committed — it is a ~100 MB binary, and which one you want is a per-machine choice.
/.godot-bin/

# Compiled golden-trace generator (the .c source + the generated golden/*.json fixtures ARE tracked)
/tools/movement-ref/movement_ref
/tools/movement-ref/movement_ref.exe

# (removed: the legacy assets/ content dir is gone — content lives in data/)
# so the Godot editor skips this tree (the game reads it via its own VFS, not Godot's

# OS
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
.DS_Store

# Dev launch log artifacts: redirected stdout from scripted game runs (_play_session*.log,
# _portaldbg*.log, _botperf*.log, …). Prefer _scratch/ for new ones; the underscore prefix keeps
# strays out of the repo either way. (The profiler's own session logs go to ~/XonData/logs.)
_*.log

# Local agent/design memory notes — kept on disk, never versioned
/memory/

# Python bytecode from the tools/ scripts (perf-report, wobble-detect, find-cvars, …)
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# Compiled maps are fetched per data/maps.lock.json, never committed (restructure D7).
data/maps/

# Fetched engine export templates (tools/data/fetch-engine-template.py, pinned by
# tools/engine-patches/engine.lock.json). Build inputs, not source — ~300 MB across three platforms.
tools/engine-templates/

# ./vx task runner build output (tools/vx/). The shim rebuilds it on demand; never committed.
/tools/vx/bin/
/tools/vx/obj/

# Neural-bot training artifacts (planning/neural-bot-lab-migration.md, step 6). A checkpoint is the output
# of a training run, not source: one stage-6 checkpoint is hundreds of MB and a run writes many of them.
# Policies are published through releases/object storage with checksums, never committed.
#
# `runs/` is deliberately unanchored: train.py --out defaults to a RELATIVE "runs", so it lands wherever
# the trainer was launched from, and the protective read is the one worth having. Nothing tracked today
# matches any of these patterns — when the cross-contract schema fixture arrives it will be a small,
# deliberately tracked .vxpw, and it gets a `!` negation for that exact path rather than loosening these.
runs/
*.pt
*.ckpt
*.vxpw

# Points the trainer at a built env host (va_neural/env.py, VX_NEURAL_HOST's file equivalent). Untracked for
# the same reason as /override.cfg above: it holds a path that is true on one machine, and a machine-specific
# path riding along in someone else's commit is exactly what a separate file exists to prevent.
neural-host.json
